\description{
Summarizes \emph{one} continuous outcome across the levels of \emph{several}
categorical variables, one block of rows per variable. It is the
inverse layout of \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}, which puts several outcomes
in rows and one grouping in columns.

Each block reports the outcome's statistics level by level, plus its
own group comparison on the block's header row, and an \code{Overall} row
gives the marginal summary of the whole analytic sample.
}
\section{Which shape do I need?}{
Several continuous variables across \emph{one} grouping is
\code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}} (\verb{select = }, \verb{by = }). One continuous variable
across one or several groupings is this function. A single \code{select} is
legitimate here -- it is the natural way in when you know more
groupings are coming -- but with several outcomes and one grouping,
the sibling is the table you want.
}

\section{Choosing the statistics}{
\code{show_columns} takes the same tokens as \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}, with
the same meanings; see the \code{show_columns} section of
\code{?table_continuous} for the vocabulary. Only the character-vector
form is accepted here: there is one outcome, so a per-variable list
would name nothing.
}

\section{Weights}{
\code{weights} applies the frequency-expansion convention of the family:
all weights 1 reproduces the unweighted table, and integer weights
reproduce the STATISTICS of the data duplicated that many times --
\code{n} stays the raw count of rows that carried the weights. Rows
with a missing or zero weight leave the analytic sample; the note
counts the missing ones.

\code{rescale} is the switch between the two readings of a weight: the
frequency reading above, and the sampling-weight reading, where the
weights are normalized to sum to the sample size. See the Weights
section of \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}} for the choice in full.

\code{rescale = TRUE} normalizes the weights over the outcome's whole
surviving sample, once, never per level -- a per-level rescale
would destroy the relative weights across levels, which is the
entire information a sampling weight carries into this table. The
means are unchanged by it; the standard deviations move, because
their denominator is \code{sum(w) - 1}.

A weighted table refuses the group comparison. The estimates and
their interval have no weighted version here, and a p-value or an
effect size silently computed unweighted beside weighted
descriptives is the one thing that must not happen: set \code{p_value = FALSE} (and \code{statistic = FALSE}, \code{effect_size = "none"}), or use
\code{\link[=table_continuous_lm]{table_continuous_lm()}} for a weighted comparison. The
order-statistic median interval is refused for the same reason.
}

\section{Blocks and the group comparison}{
Every block is a separate one-way comparison of the outcome across
the levels of that variable. Nothing in this table adjusts one block
for another, and the table note says so. Read the blocks as a set of
bivariate descriptions, not as a model.

Each block chooses its test independently: with two observed levels
\code{test = "welch"} is the Welch t-test, with three or more it is the
Welch one-way ANOVA, and \code{test = "nonparametric"} is the
Wilcoxon rank-sum or the Kruskal-Wallis test on the same rule. A
block with fewer than two observed levels, or with a level holding a
single observation, is not tested; its statistics stay empty and the
other blocks are unaffected.

A level where the outcome has no observed value (typically a question
not asked there) has no data, which is not a missing answer: its cells
show an en dash, \emph{n} included, the block compares the levels with
data, the rows of that level are not counted in the outcome's missing
values (whichever block they fall in), and the note says so ("No data
for X in G.", X the label of the outcome and G the level). The
\code{Overall} row stays the whole analytic sample.
}

\section{The \code{Overall} row}{
\code{overall = TRUE} puts the marginal summary of the whole analytic
sample on the first row. Under the default \code{drop_na = FALSE} the
levels of every block partition that sample -- the \code{(Missing)}
display level included -- so each block's counts add up to the
\code{Overall} count exactly, which is what makes it a usable
denominator.

The row reads \strong{Overall}, not \emph{Total}, and the distinction is
deliberate. \emph{Total} is the word of a COUNT margin: the column of
\code{\link[=table_categorical]{table_categorical()}} where frequencies add up. This row is the
whole analytic sample, where a mean is recomputed over every
observation and nothing is added. A mean is not a total.

A \code{select} variable displayed as \code{"Overall"} is an error (class
\code{spicy_label_collision}): choose another label in \code{labels}, or give the
row another label with
\code{options(spicy.labels = list(row_overall = "Whole sample"))}.
}

\section{Choosing the \code{select} columns}{
The canonical form is \code{select = where(is.factor)}, or an explicit
enumeration. Negation (\code{select = -c(x, y)}) is not recommended: it
sweeps in every remaining column, and a numeric one becomes a block
with one LEVEL per distinct value, in ascending order. A
variable producing more than 20 levels raises a warning for that
reason -- an arbitrary threshold, but a sixty-row block where a
reader expects a handful of categories is not a table.

A \code{haven_labelled} column used as \code{select} names its levels by their
value labels (\code{"[1] Women"}), in the order of its codes, as a labelled
\code{by} names its groups in \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}} and as the rows of a
labelled variable of \code{\link[=table_categorical]{table_categorical()}} read.
}

\section{The table note}{
One note sits under the table and states what left the analytic
sample, which group comparison ran in each block, what the displayed
columns mean, and how the blocks and the \code{Overall} row are to be
read. The rendering engines carry the same sentence as a table note.
On the \code{"tinytable"} route it is set one size down;
\code{options(spicy.note_style)} governs that (see \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}}).
}

\section{Multiple-comparison adjustment}{
The p-values of a descriptive table describe: they test no
prespecified hypothesis. STROBE (explanation of item 14) and the
SAMPL guidelines advise against significance tests in descriptive and
baseline tables, hence the default \code{p_adjust = "none"}.

When a journal or a protocol asks for adjusted p-values anyway,
\code{p_adjust} applies \code{\link[stats:p.adjust]{stats::p.adjust()}} to the family of every test in
the table: one p-value per variable (one per block in
\code{\link[=table_outcome]{table_outcome()}}), never the rows of one variable, which share one
test. \code{"holm"} suits a confirmatory set of comparisons, \code{"BH"} a
screening one; \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}} lists the methods. A test that
returned no p-value stays out of the family.

The adjustment runs before any formatting, so the \code{p} column and its
\verb{<.001} threshold show the adjusted values, and the table note gives
the number of tests and the method. The raw p-values stay in the
object: a \code{p_unadjusted} column next to the p-value column of the
returned data frames, and \code{col_meta$p$p_unadjusted} (with the method
in \code{p_adjust} and the family size in \code{p_adjust_m}) in
\code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}}. Without \code{by}, or with \code{p_value = FALSE}, the table
shows no p-value to adjust, and a \code{p_adjust} other than \code{"none"} is an
error.
}

\examples{
table_outcome(sochealth, bmi, select = c(sex, smoking))
table_outcome(sochealth, wellbeing_score, select = where(is.factor))
}
